<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Killke culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000–<a href="/years/1400">1400</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Killke culture is the archaeological culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from about 1000 to 1400 CE, named after a distinctive style of pottery. It covers the period when the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> polity was forming in the Cusco valley, and its remains include early construction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a>. It developed into the culture of the imperial Incas.</p></div>
